Alternating Subarray — DP Pattern

🧠 Core Idea

For every index i, find the longest alternating subarray starting at i.

Define:

dp[i] = longest alternating subarray starting from index i

⸻

🔑 Recurrence

Check the current element and its adjacent element:

different signs:
    dp[i] = 1 + dp[i+1]
same signs:
    dp[i] = 1

Base Case:

i == n-1
dp[i] = 1

Because a single element is always an alternating subarray.

⸻

🔍 Sign Check

Two numbers have different signs if:

(nums[i] < 0 && nums[i+1] >= 0) ||
(nums[i] >= 0 && nums[i+1] < 0)

⸻

📌 Recognition Trigger

Use this pattern when:

* Answer is required for every index
* Answer depends on the next index
* A valid sequence can be extended if the next element satisfies a condition
* You need the longest valid subarray starting from each position

Think:

“Answer at i depends on answer at i+1 → 1D DP.”

⸻

🔄 Recursive + Memoization

helper(i, i+1)
if signs are different:
    return 1 + helper(i+1, i+2)
else:
    return 1

dp[i] stores the already calculated answer for index i.

⸻

⏱ Complexity

Time  : O(N)
Space : O(N)  → DP array
         O(N)  → Recursion stack

Overall auxiliary space: O(N).

⸻

⚡ Better Iterative Pattern

Since dependency is only on i+1, recursion isn’t necessary:

ans[n-1] = 1;
for (int i = n-2; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (differentSign(A[i], A[i+1]))
        ans[i] = ans[i+1] + 1;
    else
        ans[i] = 1;
}

One-liner Pattern

If dp[i] depends only on dp[i+1], traverse from RIGHT → LEFT.
